WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.376
Moving day.

2
00:00:03.376 --> 00:00:09.951
The movers sign a receipt for each piece of furniture, and each receipt needs an inventory card of its own.

3
00:00:09.951 --> 00:00:17.141
Nobody can write that card on the first walk through the house, because the receipt didn't exist when the walk began.

4
00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:19.864
The lab has two processors.

5
00:00:19.864 --> 00:00:29.582
The receipt processor is new: for each class with an @Moving note, it writes a receipt class, LampReceipt, with the same properties plus signedBy.

6
00:00:29.582 --> 00:00:32.444
And the receipt carries an @Inventory note.

7
00:00:32.444 --> 00:00:38.701
The card processor is lesson 2's: it writes an inventory card for every class with @Inventory.

8
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:45.020
Used from code, it reads like this: make the lamp's receipt, signed by Ana, and print its card.

9
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:49.117
So here's the question a lot of us are shy to ask.

10
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:51.727
The receipt processor writes LampReceipt.

11
00:00:51.727 --> 00:00:55.420
The card processor runs right after it, in the same build.

12
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.203
Does it see the new receipt?

13
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.984
The natural guess is yes: it runs second, and the file is right there.

14
00:01:04.984 --> 00:01:07.276
Let's let the build be the referee.

15
00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:09.764
Each processor logs one line per pass.

16
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:10.741
First pass.

17
00:01:10.741 --> 00:01:12.641
Receipts wrote LampReceipt.

18
00:01:12.641 --> 00:01:15.526
Cards wrote a card for Box, and only Box.

19
00:01:15.526 --> 00:01:17.848
It walked right past the receipt.

20
00:01:17.848 --> 00:01:21.648
Only on the second pass does LampReceipt get its card.

21
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:25.874
If the card processor could talk, it would say: LampReceipt?

22
00:01:25.874 --> 00:01:27.077
Never heard of it.

23
00:01:27.077 --> 00:01:28.413
It isn't being rude.

24
00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:30.149
It can't see the file yet.

25
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.874
Picture each pass as a walk through the house.

26
00:01:33.874 --> 00:01:39.001
On a walk, the helpers take turns, one after the other, each with a fresh clipboard.

27
00:01:39.001 --> 00:01:44.861
The cards they write go on a pile by the door, and nobody reads the pile until the walk is over.

28
00:01:44.861 --> 00:01:50.720
Then, if the pile isn't empty, everyone walks again, and the new cards are new boxes to look at.

29
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:51.796
Let's map it.

30
00:01:51.796 --> 00:01:59.563
A walk is a round: one pass in which KSP calls process on every processor, in the order the services file lists them.

31
00:01:59.563 --> 00:02:06.600
The fresh clipboard is the Resolver, the object a processor asks for symbols; each round brings a new one.

32
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.517
The pile by the door is the files written during the round.

33
00:02:10.517 --> 00:02:15.031
And a new round starts only if the last one wrote at least one file.

34
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:22.318
Here's the whole build on the lab's playground: @Moving on Lamp and Van, and @Inventory on Box.

35
00:02:22.318 --> 00:02:25.611
One lane per processor, and one column per round.

36
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:28.022
Round 1 offers the source files.

37
00:02:28.022 --> 00:02:29.938
Receipts writes LampReceipt.

38
00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:35.547
Van's label mentions a LampReceipt, which doesn't exist yet, so Van can't be read.

39
00:02:35.547 --> 00:02:37.873
The processor sets that box aside.

40
00:02:37.873 --> 00:02:39.925
Cards writes the card for Box.

41
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:43.889
Round 2 offers the new files, plus the boxes set aside.

42
00:02:43.889 --> 00:02:49.147
LampReceipt exists now, so Van comes off the pile, and VanReceipt is written.

43
00:02:49.147 --> 00:02:53.039
And cards finally meets LampReceipt, and writes its card.

44
00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:57.280
Round 3: no new receipts, and VanReceipt gets its card.

45
00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.615
Round 4: nobody writes anything.

46
00:02:59.615 --> 00:03:02.972
An empty walk, so KSP stops, and calls finish.

47
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.099
Four rounds for three classes.

48
00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:09.150
If you ask the helpers, moving day has more walking than moving.

49
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:16.239
But read the shape: every output is read one round after it's written, and the last round always writes nothing.

50
00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:18.075
That's how KSP knows to stop.

51
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.083
What a round writes, the next round reads, for every processor.

52
00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:24.907
Here the picture breaks.

53
00:03:24.907 --> 00:03:29.979
Real helpers work side by side, and could pass a fresh card across the hallway.

54
00:03:29.979 --> 00:03:36.784
KSP's helpers take turns, and a card one of them writes stays invisible to the others until the next walk.

55
00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.317
To make the rounds visible, count them.

56
00:03:40.317 --> 00:03:49.512
KSP creates each processor once, so a plain field lives from round to round, and logger.warn shows the line at Gradle's normal log level.

57
00:03:49.512 --> 00:03:54.680
Keep the counter, but never keep the Resolver: next round, there's a new one.

58
00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.112
Java's annotation processors have rounds too.

59
00:03:58.112 --> 00:04:08.033
javac calls process once per round, on the same processor object, and then runs one extra, last round in which processingOver returns true.

60
00:04:08.967 --> 00:04:11.498
Setting Van aside has a name: deferral.

61
00:04:11.498 --> 00:04:17.794
The processor checks each box with validate, and returns the ones it can't read yet from process.

62
00:04:17.794 --> 00:04:19.936
KSP offers them again next round.

63
00:04:19.936 --> 00:04:23.052
The next section of the lesson takes that apart.

64
00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:24.766
And rounds aren't free.

65
00:04:24.766 --> 00:04:28.678
Every round brings a new Resolver and another validate pass.

66
00:04:28.678 --> 00:04:34.416
A chain adds a round per link: a Truck whose label mentions VanReceipt waits two rounds.

67
00:04:34.416 --> 00:04:40.936
And a file written in finish is compiled, but no round reads it, so its sticky notes are never seen.

68
00:04:41.867 --> 00:04:46.325
So, does the card processor see a receipt written in the same round?

69
00:04:46.325 --> 00:04:46.522
No.

70
00:04:46.522 --> 00:04:47.834
A round is one walk.

71
00:04:47.834 --> 00:04:51.965
What a round writes, the next round reads, for every processor.

72
00:04:51.965 --> 00:04:56.423
A box whose label mentions a card not written yet waits on the pile.

73
00:04:56.423 --> 00:04:59.571
And the walk that finds nothing new is the last.

74
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:00.557
Quick check.

75
00:05:00.557 --> 00:05:03.333
Add a Truck whose label mentions VanReceipt.

76
00:05:03.333 --> 00:05:05.793
In which round is TruckReceipt written?

77
00:05:08.967 --> 00:05:09.571
Round 3.

78
00:05:09.571 --> 00:05:14.783
Round 1 writes LampReceipt, round 2 VanReceipt, round 3 TruckReceipt.

79
00:05:14.783 --> 00:05:19.013
Its card comes in round 4, and round 5 is the empty one.
